 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "ai1",
   "source_id": "eq:Eq2",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "energy-from-ln-q",
   "prompt": "<p>A system has two states, of energy 0 and $\\varepsilon$, so $\\kQsys = 1 + e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}$. Use $\\kEavg = -(\\partial \\ln \\kQsys/\\partial\\beta)$ to find its average energy, and give $\\kEavg/\\varepsilon$ at the temperature where $k\\kT = \\varepsilon$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 0.269,
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Differentiate $\\ln(1 + e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon})$ with respect to $\\beta$, then set $\\beta\\varepsilon = 1$.",
    "solution": "<p>$\\ln \\kQsys = \\ln(1 + e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon})$, so $\\partial \\ln \\kQsys/\\partial\\beta = -\\varepsilon e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}/(1 + e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon})$ and $\\kEavg = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}}{1 + e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}} = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon} + 1}$. At $k\\kT = \\varepsilon$, $\\beta\\varepsilon = 1$ and $\\kEavg = \\varepsilon/(e + 1) = 0.269\\,\\varepsilon$. As $\\kT \\to 0$ the average falls to 0, and as $\\kT$ grows it rises toward $\\varepsilon/2$, where the two states are equally occupied.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
